=========================================================================
1. TWO SAVE MODELS ON ONE PAGE — judged, then strengthened
=========================================================================

Read BusinessClient.tsx's diff in full. Structure: "Product Catalog" card
(batch-saved category cards, unchanged) sits directly above "Products Within
Each Category" (new), both using the identical SettingsCard chrome — same
border/bg/title treatment — so the ONLY signal that the two behave
differently was text, not visual weight.

Verdict: the wording was already clear and specifically well-aimed (names
the exact button, "Save Changes," not just "the button above"), but the
banner's own visual presence was too easy to skim past — same
border-primary/20 bg-primary/5 treatment used elsewhere in this file for
low-stakes asides (e.g. AddProductForm's dashed border). Strengthened,
in-scope (CategoryProductsManager.tsx only):
  - Added a Zap icon (lucide-react, confirmed exported) — reads "instant/
    automatic," and deliberately does NOT reuse TriangleAlert/Info, which
    this codebase already uses for error/advisory states (would have
    misread as "something to worry about").
  - bg-primary/5 -> bg-primary/10, border-primary/20 -> border-primary/30,
    text-white/70 -> text-white/80 — one step up in weight each, still the
    same color family (no new palette entry).
  - Reworded the lead clause to "Products save automatically." as its own
    bolded sentence, then explains what that means and what's different.

Considered and explicitly REJECTED: tinting the "Products Within Each
Category" SettingsCard's own border via a className prop
(e.g. `<SettingsCard className="border-accent/20">`). SettingsCard
(SettingsForm.tsx, out of scope) concatenates className with template-string
interpolation, not `cn()`/tailwind-merge — despite tailwind-merge being an
installed project dependency used elsewhere (src/lib/utils.ts). Passing a
second, conflicting `border-*-color` utility this way has an outcome that
depends on Tailwind's internal generated-CSS ordering, not simply "the
later class in the string wins" the way inline styles would — I was not
willing to gamble a silently-inconsistent result for a marginal visual gain
when the in-component banner fix above already does the real work. Flagging
this as a real, if narrow, footgun in SettingsCard itself for whoever next
touches it with a conflicting className — not blocking, not this ticket's
file to fix.

Simulated the actual failure mode asked about ("would an editor ever think
they must click Save Changes after adding a product, or the reverse?"):
adding a product never touches BusinessClient's `data` state at all (the
component's own header comment states this, and CategoryProductsManager.tsx
imports nothing from settings-actions.ts — verified by reading its full
import block) — so there is no code path where staying on this page with an
unsaved product could be lost by a stray Save Changes click, or vice versa.
The reverse direction (adding a product, then believing category-card edits
also auto-saved) is exactly what the strengthened banner now states up
front, before the editor reaches the category picker.

=========================================================================
2. SAVE-ON-BLUR FEEDBACK — real bug found and fixed (client-side only)
=========================================================================

Confirmed the dispatch's description exactly: ProductRow.persist(), on a
failed save, called setDraft() back to `product`'s last-CONFIRMED values —
discarding whatever the editor had just typed, with only a small red line
below as the only trace anything happened. Agreed this is the wrong
behavior for this audience (VISION.md Target User 2 — non-technical) and
fixed it:
  - persist()'s failure branch no longer resets `draft`. The typed value
    stays on screen, stays editable, and (since ProductRow's onBlur handler
    compares draft against `product`'s last-known value) a second blur off
    the same field will retry on its own.
  - Added an explicit "Retry" text-button inside the error message itself
    (not just relying on an incidental second blur) — calls persist() again
    with the current draft, no overrides. No server-contract change: same
    updateCategoryProduct call, same CategoryProductInput shape.
  - Added a "Saving..."/"Saved" indicator (role="status" aria-live="polite",
    kept permanently mounted so screen readers pick up the busy->saved
    transition as a content change in an existing region, not a region
    appearing from nothing) — the prior implementation's only success signal
    was a bare aria-hidden spinner disappearing, with nothing for a user who
    looks away mid-save and nothing at all for a screen-reader user. "Saved"
    auto-clears after 2000ms (cleared on unmount too, via a ref + effect
    cleanup — checked this doesn't leak a pending setTimeout across a
    delete/unmount).
  - Applies uniformly to name, description, AND image (all three funnel
    through the same persist()), so an image that uploaded successfully but
    failed to attach to the row is no longer silently discarded either.

Checked for a race this change could introduce (two blur-triggered saves
overlapping): both the Name and Description inputs already share one
`busy` boolean and both are `disabled={busy}` — a user cannot blur/edit the
OTHER field while a save from THIS field is in flight, so saves are already
serialized per row. No new guard needed.

Not touched, correctly: AddProductForm's own error handling already keeps
the typed name/description/image on failure (never had this bug) — its
retry path is just clicking "Add product" again, already clear.

