4. ROW CONTROLS — keyboard order, focus rings, aria-labels, focus retention
=========================================================================

Keyboard order: sequential DOM order (image field -> Name -> Description,
controls cluster before them in the header), no tabindex overrides anywhere
in the file. No issue found.

Focus-visible rings: the up/down/delete buttons had ZERO focus styling of
any kind (no focus: classes at all — not even the border-color-only
treatment TextInput/the raw inputs elsewhere in this file use). Added
`focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-accent
focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-surface` — this exact
utility string already exists verbatim in this codebase (src/app/(website)/
(marketing)/what-we-do/page.tsx and .../[category]/page.tsx), so this reuses
an established pattern rather than inventing one; ring-offset-surface (not
-background) because the admin shell paints #0a0a0a, matching the reasoning
already on record from the T-012 pass. Captured a GENUINE :focus-visible
ring via real Tab-key CDP events (not a hand-applied class) —
04-focus-ring-1024.png — "tab-to-target: matched" confirms real keyboard
navigation actually reached the target button.

Deliberately NOT extended to the plain text inputs/select in this same file
(Name/Description/category picker), which still use the established
border-color-only focus treatment shared with TextInput/TextArea elsewhere
on this exact page. Judgment call: the dispatch's own item 4 scopes the
focus-ring ask to "row controls" specifically; the plain inputs mirror
SettingsForm.tsx's own convention verbatim (out of scope to change), and
giving only SOME inputs on this one page a ring while the visually-adjacent
"Our Craft"/"Product Catalog" cards' identical-looking inputs stay ring-less
would be a fresh, page-local inconsistency, not a fix. Recommend folding
into T-011 if the client's actual usage surfaces a real complaint.

aria-labels: were generic ("Move product up") — now interpolate the
product's own name ("Move Basic Crew Tee up", "Delete Polo Fit Tee"), with a
"this product" fallback for a row a staffer has edited down to an empty
name (no server-side non-empty check on this field, confirmed by reading
sanitizeInput in products-actions.ts — it only trims). Not verifiable by
screenshot (attributes aren't visually rendered) — confirmed by reading the
compiled source directly instead of claiming a screenshot proves it.

Disabled states at list ends: already correct before this pass
(index === 0 / index === total - 1, both combined with reorderBusy || busy)
— reconfirmed, not changed. Visible in 03-desktop-1024.png, Case 3: Product
1's up-button and Product 5's down-button both render at reduced opacity.

Focus after reorder/delete — genuine gap found and fixed:
  - DELETE: the clicked Delete button is removed from the DOM the instant
    its row is filtered out of state. A focused element removed from the DOM
    sends focus to <body> (standard browser behavior) — a keyboard user's
    very next Tab would restart from the top of the page. Fixed by computing
    the deleted row's neighbor (the row that slides into its old position,
    or the new-last row if it was last, or null if it was the only row)
    BEFORE filtering, then imperatively focusing that neighbor's own Delete
    button afterward (via a small productId -> button ref map collected
    from each ProductRow) — or the category <select> if the list is now
    empty. The target button already exists in the DOM at the moment focus
    is requested (only the DELETED row's node goes away), so this doesn't
    depend on waiting for a re-render.
  - REORDER: a subtler version of the same problem. Every row's up/down
    buttons share one `reorderBusy` flag, so clicking "move up" disables the
    clicked button itself for the whole in-flight round trip — and a
    browser blurs a focused element the instant it becomes disabled. Once
    the request resolves and reorderBusy clears, focus does NOT return on
    its own. Fixed with a small pending-focus queue: the direction + moved
    product's id is recorded at click time; a useEffect waits for
    reorderBusy to actually clear (so it never races the disabled attribute
    the same click just set), then refocuses that row's own up/down button —
    falling back to its down/up/delete sibling if the row landed at a list
    boundary and that exact control is now disabled for a legitimate reason
    (e.g. moved to the very top).

