6. RESPONSIVE — 360 / 768 / 1024, measured, not just eyeballed
=========================================================================

Static repro (all 3 cases: un-bootstrapped default state, bootstrapped-
empty state, bootstrapped-populated 5-row state covering every busy/saved/
error/boundary-disabled combination) captured via headless Chrome CDP,
document.documentElement.scrollWidth vs clientWidth measured directly
(not inferred from a screenshot alone) at each width:

  360px:  scrollWidth 360  == clientWidth 360   -> no horizontal overflow
  768px:  scrollWidth 768  == clientWidth 768   -> no horizontal overflow
  1024px: scrollWidth 1024 == clientWidth 1024  -> no horizontal overflow

(First pass of this measurement caught a REAL overflow — scrollWidth 1634px
at all 3 widths — that traced entirely to this repro's own long-form
developer captions, not to the component under test; shortened those
captions and re-measured clean. Recording this so the clean numbers above
aren't taken on faith: the tooling did catch a real overflow once, on
scratch content, which is why the component's own numbers are trustworthy.)

No `truncate`/`whitespace-nowrap`/fixed-pixel-width sits anywhere in
CategoryProductsManager.tsx's own markup (confirmed by reading the file, not
just by this one sample screenshot) — every row uses flex-col stacking for
its fields and flex-wrap-free but short, bounded-content flex rows for its
header/controls cluster (see width math in the dispatch's own item 4
discussion: label + status text + 3 icon buttons comfortably fits a 328px
content area after card padding at 360px).

